


DATE(1)             UNIX Programmer's Manual                DATE(1)



NAME
     date - print and set the date

SYNOPSIS
     date [ -u ] [ [[[yy]mm]dd]hhmm[.ss] ]

DESCRIPTION
     If no arguments are given, the current date and time are
     printed, with the time zone's abbreviation.  Providing an
     argument will set the desired date; only the super-user can
     set the date.  The kernel keeps UTC and writes the IIgs
     battery clock in local time (see _a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z(8)).

     The time given and the time printed are local time in the
     time zone TZ names, else /etc/localtime (see _c_t_i_m_e(3)).
     The kernel timezone structure is obsolete, as in 2.11BSD
     since patch #454: _d_a_t_e neither reads nor sets it.

     The -_u flag is used to display or set the date in GMT
     (universal) time.

     This is the 2.11BSD _d_a_t_e adapted to FGX.  There is no
     _t_i_m_e_d(8) network time daemon (-_n is gone; setting is
     always local), and no _w_t_m_p or _s_y_s_l_o_g logging.

     _y_y_m_m_d_d_h_h_m_m[._s_s] is interpreted as follows: _y_y is the year
     (last two digits; values before 70 mean the next century),
     _m_m the month, _d_d the day, _h_h the hour (24-hour clock), _m_m
     the minute, and ._s_s an optional seconds field.  Trailing
     components may be omitted from the left: giving only _h_h_m_m
     sets the time within the current day.

EXAMPLES
     date 2607111845.30

     sets the date to July 11, 2026, 6:45:30 PM.

FILES
     The clock is the IIgs battery RAM real-time clock, written
     via the Miscellaneous Tool Set.  The year must be 1904 or
     later.

SEE ALSO
     gettimeofday(2), settimeofday(2), ctime(3), adjkerntz(8)

BUGS
     Near a change to or from summer time, a time set within the
     zone's offset of the change can be an hour out: the offset
     used is the one in force at the time given read as UTC, as
     in 2.11BSD's _d_a_t_e.

